It was 3.37am when Hannah got back to Palacio Tangara. She’d been able to walk back with a few of the Mclaren senior engineers who were also staying there. She liked them enough - they had been drowning their sorrows of a double DNF earlier in the day. Hannah had spent a lot of the walk back trying to suss out what exactly was going on with Daniel - he was brash, and could be annoying when matters turned serious but she’d liked him enough. It hadn’t been announced yet but he was coming back to them as a reserve driver, but it seemed like the glimmer in his eyes had died. She’d heard rumours, everyone had - but he was one of Christian’s favourite sons, he’d pontificated endlessly that it was all Zak’s fault. Nothing to do with the cracks they’d started to see before he left for Renault.
She threw her bag on the large double bed, she needed a shower - was the sweat from her skin. Folding her clothes next to the ordered suitcase, she felt guilty at enjoying it so much. No toys strewn everywhere, no food smeared on the wall, and no laundry piled high and ignored until she returned home. Jonas really should be grateful she’s away so often, she thinks to herself. Not going completely insane as she was able to relax somewhere quiet and clean… at least in the few moments she’d get when she wasn’t pulled into meetings, or hunched over her desk in the Red Bull motorhome. But she needed that too - the fight. The rush. The hunt. Something that makes her feel alive again. Not that children and family life didn’t. It just never matched up to the adrenaline hit she’d grown accustomed to in the 13 year career she’d built. 
She avoids looking at her body in the large bathroom mirror, it was far too late and she was far too tired for the meltdown she’d have if she did. Hannah knew her figure had changed, her mother cruelly pinched at the roles of her stomach every time she visited. but she barely recognised herself sometimes. Only at work did she ever feel something close to the old self she missed desperately. The pads of her fingers mindlessly run across the silver striations on her stomach as the shower comes up to temperature. 

